---
import AppLayout from '../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import ChartSimulator from '../components/chartsimulator/ChartSimulator.tsx';
---

<AppLayout
  title="Candlestick Pattern Simulator | Stockade"
  description="Learn candlestick patterns with our free interactive chart simulator. Explore 20+ patterns: Doji, Hammer, Engulfing, Morning Star & more."
>
  <h1 class="sr-only">Candlestick Pattern Simulator</h1>
  <ChartSimulator client:load />

  <section
    slot="below"
    id="how-to-use"
    class="border-t border-[var(--c-border)] bg-[var(--c-bg-soft)]"
  >
    <div class="max-w-[820px] mx-auto px-6 py-12">
      <details class="group" open>
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
        >
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--c-text)]">
            How to Use the Chart Simulator
          </h2>
          <svg
            class="shrink-0 text-[var(--c-text-subtle)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
            width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" />
          </svg>
        </summary>

        <div class="mt-6">
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The Chart Simulator is a candlestick replay tool. It builds a fresh synthetic price series
            every time you load it, scans that series for classic candlestick patterns, and marks each
            one on the chart — so you can watch a setup form bar by bar rather than meeting it already
            finished in a textbook diagram.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            Running a replay
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            Press play — or hit the spacebar — to reveal candles one at a time. The amber progress bar
            under the toolbar shows how far through the series you are. The speed buttons run the replay
            at 1×, 2×, 5× or 10×. To move manually, use the step-back and step-forward arrows or the
            <kbd class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[#f59e0b]">←</kbd> and
            <kbd class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[#f59e0b]">→</kbd> keys, and the skip buttons to jump
            straight to the start or the end. The refresh button generates a new chart with a different
            set of patterns.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            Choosing what to plot
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The symbol dropdown covers stocks, crypto, forex and futures, and the timeframe buttons
            re-aggregate the same series into longer or shorter bars. Overlay EMA 9, EMA 20, EMA 50 or
            VWAP from the toolbar, and switch the lower pane between volume, RSI and MACD.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            What to look for
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The Patterns panel on the left lists every formation the simulator recognises — Doji, Hammer,
            Bullish and Bearish Engulfing, Harami, Morning and Evening Star, Three White Soldiers, Tweezer
            tops and bottoms, and more. Click a pattern to highlight only its occurrences, or click a marker
            on the chart to open its explanation, including the market psychology behind the setup and what
            confirms or invalidates it. The most useful habit: pause on a marker, predict the next candle,
            then step forward and check. Context matters more than the shape — a reversal pattern only
            means something after a real trend, and volume behind the signal candle separates a genuine
            turn from noise.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppLayout>
